From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:31:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J: a stream or an angle selected before a playlist player
 has a play item is kept for its start

The stream controls of a playlist player (SubtitlingControl,
PrimaryAudioControl, SecondaryAudioControl) check a selection against the
streams of the play item being presented, which a player has only once it
is prefetched. Before that, selectStreamNumber(), selectSubtitle() and
selectLanguage() refused any number with StreamNotAvailableException (or
NotAuthorizedException), and AngleControl.selectAngle() went to whatever
playlist libbluray had open, or failed: a disc that sets the streams of
a playlist while it prepares the player lost them.

A selection made before the player has a play item now goes into the
locator the player starts from, as a seek made before it does
(PlaylistPlayerAction): doPrefetch() writes it to its register before it
starts the playlist, where libbluray keeps it if the first play item has
that stream and otherwise chooses by language, as for any stream
register at a play item. It is accepted when a play item of the playlist
has a stream of that number (selectLanguage(): the number of that
language in the first play item that has it); a number that no play
item has is refused, as before. An angle is kept when the playlist has
it and selected once the playlist is open. Once the player is
prefetched, a selection is one of the play item, as before, also when a
later play item would have the stream. The standard error of the BD-J
host says each selection that is kept.

Checked with a host test of the classes (JDK 8, the jar built with the
command of the image's build): before the player is prefetched, a stream
of the second play item is kept in the locator for subtitles, audio and
secondary audio, a number no play item has is refused, the locator is
left as it was, an angle the playlist has is kept and is not sent to
libbluray, and once prefetched the selection reaches libbluray as before
and the player keeps nothing (23 checks; nine mutations of the change
each fail).
